22:53:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a totally fair question—and you’re not alone. Many, many people were taught exactly what your teacher said, sometimes even with pictures in textbooks. So it’s understandable that it sticks with you. But yes, your teacher was (unintentionally) repeating a very common **scientific urban legend** that was widely believed for decades. Here’s how that happened, and why the story changed. --- ### 1. It really *was* taught as fact for a long time The idea that glass is a “supercooled liquid” and cathedral windows are proof of flow can be traced back to the 19th and early 20th centuries. Some prominent physicists and chemists speculated about it, and the “thicker at the bottom” observation was simply too tempting a story to check. It made its way into textbooks and popular science books long before the data said otherwise. By the mid-to-late 20th century, materials science advanced enough to show the misconception, but many high school curricula and even college intro courses carried the old narrative for years. Your teacher very likely believed it was true because that’s what *they* were taught. --- ### 2. Why the pictures don’t prove flow The photos show real, old windows—often from medieval cathedrals—where panes are visibly thicker at the bottom and thinner at the top. But as I mentioned before, this is a **manufacturing artifact**, not deformation: - Crown glass (spun glass) produced discs that were thicker at the edge and thinner at the center. When cut into panes, the pieces were wedge-shaped. - Glaziers intentionally mounted the heavier edge down for stability, and the fashion of the time didn’t demand perfectly uniform glass. - In some windows, you can even find panes installed *sideways* or *thick-edge up* — clear evidence they didn’t sag after installation. If glass flowed, we’d never see a thick-top pane unless it had been installed upside down much later. So the photos are real, but the *interpretation* of “flow” has been completely overturned. --- ### 3. How do we *know* your teacher was wrong? Modern measurements have nailed this down: - The viscosity of soda-lime glass at room temperature is about **10²⁰ Pa·s** (or ~10²¹ poise). That’s 10 billion billion times stiffer than pitch, the famous “slow-flowing liquid” in the pitch-drop experiment. - Physicists have calculated that if glass flowed enough to cause the observed thickness difference (say, a few millimeters in 1,000 years), the room-temperature viscosity would need to be about 10¹³ times lower than it actually is. - We also have **Roman glass objects** nearly 2,000 years old that show no measurable flow. If glass really crept at room temperature, those should be visibly deformed. --- ### 4. Why might a chemistry teacher still teach it? A few reasons: - **It’s a great story.** It connects a visible curiosity to a counterintuitive property of matter. It’s memorable, and memorable things get passed on. - **Outdated resources.** Many high school textbooks reprinted the myth for decades; some might still mention it today with a caution, but not all do. - **Confusion of states of matter.** The “supercooled liquid” label for glass isn’t entirely wrong *at a molecular level*—glass *is* structurally similar to a liquid—but it’s misleading because the material behaves as a brittle solid over all practical (and impractical) timescales. Teachers sometimes simplify this to “glass is a liquid,” skipping crucial quantitative reality. --- ### 5. How to think about it now You’ve essentially asked one of the classic examples of a persistent science myth that got corrected. Your teacher wasn’t alone—this was taught to generations. The fact that you’re questioning it now shows you’re thinking like a scientist, which is exactly the goal. So in short: your teacher showed you real photos and gave you the explanation that was standard at one time. But modern physics and materials science have soundly proved that no, cathedral windows don’t ooze, and glass is an amorphous solid that doesn’t flow on any timescale we can observe at room temperature.
