e ripples or slight thickness variations. Those aren’t flow, either; they’re from the sheet-drawing or rolling processes. Time hasn’t changed them. A 1920s window with slight waves today looks exactly like it did when it was installed. --- ### So your observation is sharp, but it’s pointing at manufacturing, not flow You saw a genuine correlation: older glass = wedge-shaped, newer glass = flat. That’s a good piece of detective work. But the underlying cause isn’t time acting on the material; it’s the history of technology. Once float glass arrived, the wedge disappeared overnight. If the wedge were due to slow flow, we’d expect modern glass to be slowly developing it, too—but it isn’t, even after decades. I get why it feels like time is the culprit. The evidence just points a different direction once you know what’s behind the curtain. And now you do.
